<activity label="test-library-code">
  <statement>
    <p>Why should you test code from a library or other existing code after adding it to your program?</p>
  </statement>

  <choices randomize="yes">
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>Library code is always correct.</p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Library functions are generally designed to work
          correctly, but you should still test them in your program.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>Testing makes it faster.</p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. Testing does not make the code
          run faster.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The code may behave differently than you expected.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          Correct! Testing helps you make sure that the reused code
          works as expected in your program.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>

    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>It won't work without testing.</p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>
          Incorrect. It will probably work, but you should still test it to make sure.
        </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>
  
    </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="API-project">
  <title>API Project</title>

    <idx><h>projects</h><h>dog API</h></idx>
    <idx><h>coding challenge</h><h>dog API</h></idx>

  <p>In this project, you will use an API For a web service called <url href="https://dog.ceo/dog-api">
  https://dog.ceo/dog-api</url> which provides free dog images by breed. In the documentation,
  <url href="https://dog.ceo/dog-api/documentation/random">https://dog.ceo/dog-api/documentation/random</url>, you can find
  the url (web address) for fetching random dog images. You can also try it in your browser to see  
  that <url href="https://dog.ceo/api/breeds/image/random">https://dog.ceo/api/breeds/image/random</url>
 returns some text like <c>{"message":"https://images.dog.ceo/breeds/german-shepherd/n02106662_16342.jpg","status":"success"}</c> and if we paste the embedded image link into a browser, 
  we can see the image that it returns <url href="https://images.dog.ceo/breeds/german-shepherd/n02106662_16342.jpg">
  https://images.dog.ceo/breeds/german-shepherd/n02106662_16342.jpg</url>. Try 
  running the active code below to see the random images that it returns which is
  displayed in the web page using a hidden <c>showImage</c> function.
</p>

<p>Now, take a look at the documentation for showing random dog images of a certain breed at <url href="https://dog.ceo/dog-api/breeds-list">https://dog.ceo/dog-api/breeds-list</url>. Pull down the menu to see different breeds.</p>

<p>Let's use this API to fetch images of a specific dog breed that the user chooses. 
  First, create a list of at least 5 dog breeds that are in the API. Then, use a loop to 
  print the list of dog breeds with their index number. Then, ask the user to input the index number of the dog breed they want to see. 
  Finally, use the API to fetch a random image of that breed and display it on the web page using the <c>showImage</c> function. 
</p>

<project label="projectAPI">
  <title>Dog API Project</title>
  <statement>
    <p>Run the code below to see a random dog image from the dog API.</p>
    <p>Then, change it to show different breeds. </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Create a list of at least 5 dog breeds that are in 
      <url href="https://dog.ceo/dog-api/breeds-list">https://dog.ceo/dog-api/breeds-list</url>.</li>
      <li>Use an indexed for loop to print the list of dog breeds with their index number.</li>
      <li>Ask the user to input the index number of the dog breed they want to see.</li>
      <li>Use the breeds list and the user's index input to save the selected breed name.</li>
      <li>Use the selected breed name to fetch a random image using the APIString "https://dog.ceo/api/breed/" + breed + "/images/random"  </li>
      <li>Change the function apiDog and its function call to have the selected breed as an argument</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
  <preamble visible="no">
import document

def showImage(image):
    #id = "cspossible__projectAPI_graphics"
    # for local testing
    id = "rs-projectAPI_graphics"
    output = document.getElementById(id)
    output.innerHTML = ""
    img = document.createElement("img")
    img.src = image
    img.width = 400
    output.appendChild(img)
  </preamble>
  <code>
import requests

# Run once to see a random dog image from the dog API
# You will edit this function and its function call in step 6 below
def apiDog():
    apiString = "https://dog.ceo/api/breeds/image/random"
    data = requests.get(apiString).json()
    print(data)
    showImage(data["message"])

# TODO: 
# 1. Create a list of at least 5 dog breeds that are in the API https://dog.ceo/dog-api/breeds-list

# 2. Use an indexed for-loop to print the list of dog breeds with their index number

# 3. Ask the user to input the index number of the dog breed they want to see (convert to int)

# 4. Use the breeds list and the user's index input to save the selected breed name

# 5. Change the function apiDog and its function call to have the selected breed as an argument

apiDog()

# 6. In the apiDog function above use the argument selected breed name to fetch a random image 
# Hint: Create an APIString that has "https://dog.ceo/api/breed/", the breed, and "/images/random"

  </code>
 <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
        self.assertIn("=[", code, "Define a list of breeds")
        self.assertIn("for ", self.getEditorText(), "Use an indexed for loop with range to print the list of breeds with their index number")
        self.assertIn("input(", code, "Ask the user for input")
        self.assertIn('\"https://dog.ceo/api/breed/\"+', code, "Add the selected breed to the APIString")
        self.assertNotIn("apiDog()", code, "Change function to apiDog(breed) with the selected breed as an argument")

myTests().main()
 </tests>
  </program>
</project>

  <activity label="frq-data-api-abstraction">
        <title>Data and API Abstraction Reflection</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the list and the API used in your project code above. 
          </p>
